- op: discuss  # c9a2
  at: '2026-07-31T16:39:58Z'  # c9a2
  by: agent  # c9a2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c9a2
  clock: 3  # c9a2
  nonce: c9a2  # c9a2
  message: "RESOLVED. Both limbs fixed; the G1 ratchet is green on all four substrates.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (the item's own filed repro, re-run):\n\n  before:  pytest packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_validation_report_empty.py\n           AssertionError: [default] G1 ratchet breached ...\n             runtime_coherence: 2 un-allow-listed offenders > baseline 1:\n               ('file','rust','external_symbol','rust') types=['imports','module_attr_ref'] edges=3\n               ('trait','rust','method','rust')         types=['contains','dispatches_to']  edges=2\n           1 failed\n\n  after:   4 passed\n\n  before:  BRANCHES_test_go.py::TestGoRouteExtraction::test_route_handler_as_selector\n           assert any(\"GetUser\" in n[\"name\"] for n in routes)  ->  assert False\n  after:   1 passed\n\nFull run: 1752 passed, 1 skipped, 100% coverage; all 6 packages 100% in isolation.\nBoth tests are now IN the committed manifest (.ci/affected-tests.txt lines 51 and\n55), so CI actually selects them — which is the half of this item that mattered\nmost.\n\n(a) WHAT PRODUCES THE EDGE — measured, not inferred.\n\nChecked out 9baaa9afbd^ (parent of WI-duguk \"emit enum variants and trait\nmembers\"), re-ran the corpus, restored:\n\n    OFFENDERS: 1\n      ('file','rust','external_symbol','rust') ['imports','module_attr_ref'] edges=3\n\nSo WI-duguk introduced the second offender. `function_signature_item` ->\nkind=\"method\" named \"MyTrait::method\" gave the containment linker a member to\nroot, so the trait now `contains` its own requirement, while\nlinkers/rust_trait_dispatch (WI-kivut) was already emitting `dispatches_to` from\nthe trait to the impl's method. Two edges, same endpoint shape, DIFFERENT\ndestinations: contains -> MyTrait::method, dispatches_to -> MyStruct::method.\n\nThis corrects the item's own framing on two points. It is not \"unclear what was\nproducing it\" — it is WI-duguk, provably. And \"pre-existing, not introduced by\nthis session\" was right about the session and wrong about the change: the edge\npredates the session that FOUND it, not the change that CAUSED it. Same\nscope-axis error the item's parent (WI-duguk) was itself filed with.\n\n(b) RULING: LEGITIMATE.\n\nDistinct relationships over one endpoint shape, not an endpoint property leaking\ninto edge_type — the exact reasoning that admitted the file-anchor entries.\nAdmitted via an ADR-0023 §3 amendment + allow-list entry.\n\nRemedy 1 (raise the baseline) stays refused: shrink-only means shrink-only.\nRemedy 2 was taken, but NARROWLY — the entry rules on the partition and states in\nwriting that it does not ratify the anchoring that made it necessary, so the\nfollow-up fix SHRINKS the allow-list rather than contradicting it.\nRemedy 3 (fix the producer) is correct but is not this item; see below.\n\nThe \"governance-touching\" objection that blocked remedy 2 last session was\nFALSE. Neither docs/adr/** nor docs/edge-type-runtime-allowlist.yaml is on the\nAGENTS.md governance list. That in-test sentence is the entire reason the breach\nlooked unfixable in-band; it has been replaced with an accurate note.\n\n(c) ROOT CAUSE IS ONE LEVEL DEEPER — filed as INV-tihim (P1).\n\nlinkers/type_hierarchy is the framework-agnostic linker that emits\ninterface-method -> impl-method dispatches_to. It CANNOT FIRE FOR RUST:\n\n    _get_method_short_name('Animal.speak')        -> 'speak'           owner='Animal'\n    _get_method_short_name('UserController#index')-> 'index'           owner='UserController'\n    _get_method_short_name('MyTrait::method')     -> 'MyTrait::method' owner=None\n\nIt splits on \"#\" and \".\" but not \"::\", while linkers/containment's _SEPARATORS =\n(\"#\", \"::\", \".\") knows all three. One fact -- how a language qualifies a member\nname onto its owner -- recorded in two places that disagree. rust_trait_dispatch\nexists to paper over that gap, and anchors at the trait because trait members did\nnot exist when it was written. Its docstring said so explicitly; WI-duguk made\nthat statement false and it has now been corrected in place rather than left to\nmislead the next reader (the L50 shape).\n\nCorroborated on the same corpus run -- every other language ALREADY uses the\nmethod->method anchoring, and Rust is the sole outlier:\n\n    (interface, csharp|go|java|typescript) -> method   ['contains']\n    (method,    csharp|go|java|typescript) -> method   ['dispatches_to']\n    (trait,     rust)                      -> method   ['contains','dispatches_to']   <-- outlier\n\nHonest caveat recorded in the ADR: those method->method partitions are\nsingle-valued on THIS fixture corpus, which has no intra-language method calls.\nOn a substrate that has them, (method,L)->(method,L) would carry `calls`\nalongside `dispatches_to` and need its own ruling -- for every language. So \"the\nfollow-up deletes this allow-list entry\" is conditional, not guaranteed.\n\n(d) GO ROUTE TEST — fixed, and the item's proposed fix was wrong.\n\nThe item said to read meta[\"handler_ref\"]. Measured: the go path emits\nmeta[\"handler_name\"] = \"h.GetUser\" (handler_ref is what OTHER producers use).\nAsserts on handler_name now. Every other route-name assertion in the tree\nalready reads the post-WI-zugob \"{METHOD} {path}\" convention, so this was the\nonly straggler, consistent with the WI-zugob changelog's \"exactly two\nassertions\" count.\n\nNOT DONE, deliberately: RUNTIME_COHERENCE_BASELINES stays at 1 rather than\nratcheting to 0. The surviving (file,rust)->(external_symbol,rust) offender has\nnever been ruled on, and allow-listing an unexamined partition is the same\nmistake this item refused to make for the trait one. It is declared in-test with\nan accurate note."  # c9a2
